{ You are Black, with a knight and two pawns against his rook. The fork: watch where his rook goes, then go hunting for one. }
58. Rh1 { His rook goes into the corner to attack your edge pawn, and that is the mistake: a rook in a corner is a target. Where can your knight hit two things at once? [%csl Gf5] } 58... Ng3+ { Check, and the rook in the corner is attacked as well. He has to answer the check, so that rook is not allowed to run. [%cal Gg3e4,Gg3h1] } (58... h4 { You push the pawn and let the fork slip away, because with no check on the board his rook is free to walk off. } 59. Rg1 Ne7 60. Kf4 { His rook has slipped away from your knight and nothing of his is loose. The position is dead level. [%csl Gg1] }) (58... Nd6+ { A check, yes, but look at what else that knight touches: his rook is nowhere near any of it. } 59. Kf3 { His king simply steps aside and you have gained nothing. A check with only one target is just a check. [%csl Rh1] }) (58... Kg5 { Your king steps up, but his rook wakes up at once and checks you. } 59. Rg1+ Kf6 60. Rf1 { His rook is out of the corner and attacks your knight. Nothing of his hangs, and the position is dead level. [%csl Rf1] }) (58... Nh6 { You tuck the knight away, and the rook in the corner is no longer in any danger. } 59. Ra1 { His rook strolls out of the corner. The fork is gone, and the position is dead level. [%csl Ra1] }) 59. Kf4 { Every move he had was a king step, and no piece of his can come and guard that corner. Go and collect the rook. } 59... Nxh1 { A whole rook, and you gave nothing for it. That is a fork: two targets at once, and he could only rescue one. } (59... Ne2+ { You rescue your knight and forget the rook, and a fork you never collect was worth nothing at all. } 60. Ke3 Ng3 61. Ra1 { His rook has left the corner and your knight has nothing left to bite on. Neither side can make progress. [%csl Ra1] }) (59... g5+ { Check, but look at the knight you leave behind: his king simply eats it. } 60. Kxg3 h4+ 61. Kg4 { Your knight is gone and his rook is still alive. A rook against two pawns: he is winning. [%csl Rg3] }) (59... h4 { The pawn runs, and the rook that was yours for the taking runs too. } 60. Re1 Nf5 61. Kg4 { His rook got out, his king is chasing your pawns, and the position is dead level. [%csl Ge1] }) 0-1


