{ You are White. A busy board: pieces everywhere, and his pawn is attacking your bishop. He thinks he is about to win a piece. [%csl Rd5] }
32... dxe4 { His pawn takes your bishop, and now he is a knight up for a pawn. Taking was his mistake: he had to take your knight first, with his queen. } 33. Ng6+ { Check, and his queen along the same back row is attacked as well. He has to move his king, so she cannot get away. [%cal Gg6h8,Gg6f8] } (33. Nc6 { Your knight hops out to a safe square, but it attacks nothing he would mind losing. } 33... Qa8 34. Qc7 { His queen has strolled to safety, and you are left a knight down for one pawn. [%csl Ra8] }) (33. Be5 { You attack his knight, but he has a much bigger capture waiting and he takes first. } 33... Qxe7 34. Bxf6+ Qxf6 35. Qxd7 { His knight was loose, so your queen takes it back, but he stays a bishop up for a pawn. He is winning. [%csl Rd7] }) (33. Qxd7 { A free knight? No. His other knight was guarding it, and it takes your queen. [%cal Rf6d7] } 33... Nxd7 { His knight ate your queen. A queen for a knight, and he is winning. [%csl Rd7] }) (33. Qxe4 { You take the pawn back, but his knight was guarding that square too, and it takes your queen. [%cal Rf6e4] } 33... Nxe4 { Your queen is gone for a pawn. He is winning. [%csl Re4] }) (33. Bd6 { Your bishop attacks his queen, but she simply steps away, and your knight in his camp is still in trouble. } 33... Qa8 34. Qc7 Kg7 { He keeps his extra knight, and your knight deep in his camp attacks nothing at all. He is much better. [%csl Re7] }) 33... Kg8 { His king steps aside, because a check has to be answered first. His queen is still sitting where your knight can take her. } 34. Nxf8 { His queen comes off the board for your knight, and that is a bargain. Now watch him take back. [%tail] } (34. Ne5 { Your knight backs off and his queen is free again, so the fork you found is handed straight back. } 34... Qa8 35. Qc7 { You had his queen in a fork and let her walk. He keeps his extra knight and you keep nothing. [%csl Ra8] }) (34. Ne7+ { Another check, but this time his queen simply takes the knight. [%cal Rf8e7] } 34... Qxe7 { You gave a knight for nothing, and he is two pieces up for a pawn. He is winning. [%csl Re7] }) (34. Qxd7 { His other knight still guards that square, and it takes your queen. [%cal Rf6d7] } 34... Nxd7 35. Nxf8 Nxf8 { You got his queen after all, but yours went first. Both queens gone, he is a knight up for a pawn, and he is winning. [%csl Rf8] }) (34. Qc7 { Your queen attacks his knight, but his queen steps out of the fork first. } 34... Qe8 35. Ne5 Ra8 { His queen has slipped out of the fork and he keeps his extra knight. He is better. [%csl Re8] }) 34... Kxf8 35. Bd6+ { Check. His king took your knight back, but your knight had taken his queen first. One jump, two targets, and that is the fork. } 1-0


