{ You are Black, and the material is level. His queen is active, and both his rooks are still on the back row. }
17. Rc2 { His rook steps up to attack your pawn, and that is the mistake: nothing of his guards it where it stands. [%cal Rc2c6] } 17... Qg6+ { Check along the column! And along the diagonal sits the rook he just moved: your queen hits both. He has to save his king first. [%cal Gg6g1,Gg6c2] } (17... f6 { You attack his pawn, and he brings his other rook across to guard the loose one. } 18. Rfc1 fxe5 19. Qxe5 { The pawns came off, and the position is level. [%csl Gc1] }) (17... Rab8 { Your rook lines up against his pawn, and his queen simply slides across to cover the checking square. } 18. Qg4 { The check that was coming has gone, and the position is about level. [%csl Gg4] }) (17... Qxe5 { You grab the pawn in the middle, but his queen takes yours. [%cal Rf4e5] } 18. Qxe5 { A queen for a pawn. He is winning. [%csl Re5] }) (17... Qg4+ { A check, but his pawn simply takes your queen. [%cal Rf3g4] } 18. fxg4 { Your queen is gone for nothing. He is winning. [%csl Rg4] }) 18. Kh1 { His king steps into the corner, because a check must be answered first. His rook is still standing on the line your queen looks down. } 18... Qxc2 { A whole rook, and nothing of his can take your queen back. One check, two targets, and he could only save his king: that is a fork. [%csl Gc2] } (18... f6 { You attack his pawn instead of taking the rook, and the rook runs away. } 19. Rxc6 Qd3 20. Kg2 { His rook escaped and ate a pawn on the way. The position is level. [%csl Gc6] }) (18... Rae8 { Your rook comes to the middle, and his other rook comes across to guard the one you were attacking. } 19. Rfc1 { His rooks are safe and the fork is gone. The position is level. [%csl Gc1] }) (18... Qg2+ { A check, but his king just takes your queen. [%cal Rh1g2] } 19. Kxg2 { His king ate your queen, and you got nothing for her. He is winning. [%csl Rg2] }) 0-1


