{ You are Black, and we will now learn the pin. A pinned piece is stuck: if it steps aside, something bigger behind it is taken. }
16. Bxa8 { He grabs your rook, and that is his mistake: he is a rook up for a pawn, but his queen and his king sit on one diagonal and he has done nothing about it. Slide your bishop onto that diagonal, right in front of his queen. [%csl Rd2,Rc1][%cal Yc5e3] } 16... Be3 { Your bishop pins his queen against his king. She cannot step aside now, because that would leave his king in check. [%cal Ge3d2,Gd2c1] } (16... Qa7 { Your queen goes after his bishop instead, and he uses the move to step his queen off the diagonal. } 17. Qc3 Qxa8 { You win the bishop back, but a rook against a bishop and a pawn is still too much: he is winning. [%csl Rc3] }) (16... a5 { A quiet pawn push while you are a rook down, and it hands him a free move to sort his king out. } 17. Rfe1 b4 18. Kb1 { His king has stepped off that diagonal, so there is nothing left to pin and he keeps your rook. [%csl Rb1] }) 17. Qxe3 { He takes your bishop with his queen, which was the only square she was still allowed to visit. Take her back with your queen. [%cal Yb6e3] } 17... Qxe3+ { Your queen takes his, with check. You paid a rook and a bishop, he paid his queen, and you come out ahead. } (17... Qc7 { You forget to take his queen back, and your bishop has simply been given away. } 18. Bg3 { Your bishop is gone for nothing. He is a rook and a bishop up, and only one pawn went the other way. [%csl Re3] }) 0-1


