{ You are White. His queen is attacking your knight, and he is about to take it. The pin: look for a way to turn the tables. }
38... Qxg7 { His queen takes your knight, and that is the mistake: she should have gone hunting your pawns instead. Now she stands right next to his king, with nothing between them. } 39. Rf7 { Your rook pins his queen to his king. She cannot leave that row, and if she takes your rook, your other rook takes her back. [%cal Gf7g7,Gg7h7,Gf1f7] } (39. h3 { A quiet move, and his knight jumps to the middle while his queen walks free. Suddenly he is the one winning. } 39... Ne5 40. Kh2 Qc7 { His queen and knight are safe and active, and a queen against two rooks is winning for him. [%csl Re5] }) (39. R1f2 { You double the rooks, but his knight comes to the middle and his queen starts checking. } 39... Ne5 40. Rf8 Qb7+ { His queen checks and she is free for good. A queen and a knight against two rooks: he is winning. [%cal Rb7h1] }) 39... Ne5 { His knight jumps out and attacks your rook, hoping you will run. [%cal Re5f7] } 40. Rxg7+ { You take his queen with check. His king takes back, and that is the trade: a queen for a rook. Watch. [%tail] } (40. R7f4 { Your rook runs from the knight, and the pin is gone with it. } 40... Qb7+ 41. Kg1 Qg7+ { His queen is free and checking, and he is winning. [%csl Rg7] }) (40. Rf8 { Your rook steps up the column to his back row, and the pin is gone. } 40... Qb7+ 41. Kg1 Qg7+ { His queen escaped with check, and a queen against two rooks is winning for him. [%csl Rg7] }) 40... Kxg7 41. Kg2 { His king took your rook back. Count it up: a queen for a rook. She was pinned to her king and could not run: that is the pin. } 1-0


