{ You are Black, and his queen is aiming straight at your king. Defending looks urgent. [%csl Rg3] }
28. Bxh6 { His bishop takes your pawn, and that is the mistake: he is sure mate is coming next to your king, and it is, unless you find the one move that stops it. [%cal Rg3g7] } 28... Rg8 { Your rook pins his queen to his king. She cannot leave the column, so the mate is gone. [%cal Gg8g3,Gg3g1] } (28... Qg5 { You offer a queen trade, but his bishop takes your queen for nothing, and his attack on your king rolls on. } 29. Bxg5 f5 30. Qh4+ { Your queen is gone and his is hunting your king. He mates soon. [%csl Rh8] }) (28... Qf6 { You cover the square with your queen, and his pawn takes her. } 29. exf6 Rg8 30. Bg7+ { His bishop checks, and whatever takes it, his queen mates next move. [%csl Rh8] }) (28... Ne7 { A knight move, and his queen mates you at once. } 29. Qg7# { Mate. The square next to your king was guarded by his bishop. [%csl Rg7] }) 29. Kh1 { His king steps aside to break the pin, but it is your move, and his queen is still standing on the line your rook attacks. } 29... Rxg3 { You take his queen, and his pawn takes back: a queen for a rook. Watch. [%tail] } (29... Rg6 { You let her go, and she slips away with a threat of her own. } 30. Qh3 Qg8 31. Rg1 { His queen is safe and his pieces are swarming your king. He is winning. [%csl Rh6] }) (29... Kh7 { Your king steps up to attack the bishop, and his queen slides away. } 30. Qh2 Rg6 31. Bg5+ Kg7 32. Bxd8 { His bishop stepped aside with a check from his queen behind it, then took your queen. He is winning. [%csl Rd8] }) 30. fxg3 Qg8 { His pawn took your rook back. A queen for a rook, because she was pinned to her king and could not leave the column: that is the pin. Your queen now looks down that column. [%cal Gg8g3] } 0-1


