{ You are Black, and we will now learn the skewer. Check his king, and then take whatever was hiding on the line behind him. }
41. Qh1 { His queen hides behind his king, and that is a mistake: he had a check of his own with the bishop and missed it. Now both of them sit on his back row, where your rook can reach. Slide your rook there with check. [%csl Rg1,Rh1][%cal Ya5a1] } 41... Ra1+ { Check along the first row, and he has nothing but king moves. Whichever way he steps, his queen is what your rook runs into. [%cal Ga1g1,Ga1h1] } (41... Bxd4 { You grab a pawn with the bishop, and the skewer that wins his queen slips away. } 42. Qe4 Rxd5 43. Qxd5 { His queen slipped away and ate your rook on the way. A queen against your bishop and knight: he is better. [%csl Rd5] }) (41... Ra7 { Your rook steps back to safety and the skewer goes with it, because nothing is checking his king any more. } 42. Bxc4 { He grabbed a pawn for free, and with his queen and bishop raking your king he is winning. [%csl Rc4] }) 42. Kh2 { His king steps up beside his queen. Now run your rook along the row and take her. [%cal Ya1h1] } 42... Rxh1+ { You take his queen with check, and his king has to take your rook back. Watch. [%tail] } (42... Ra7 { You retreat instead of collecting, and his queen strolls out of the corner alive. } 43. Qb1 { The skewer has gone for good and his queen is safe. He is winning, and you have nothing to show. [%csl Gb1] }) 43. Kxh1 Bd8 { Your bishop drops back towards the red pawn that nothing guards. Count it up: a rook for a queen, and you are a knight up for a pawn. That is the skewer: check the king, then take what was hiding behind him. [%csl Rc3] } 0-1


