{ You are White, and his pawns have marched deep into your camp. The material is level, but one careless king step can change everything. The skewer: find it. }
34... Kf6 { His king steps forward to help his pawns, and that is the mistake: now his king and his rook stand on the same diagonal. [%csl Rf6,Rd8] } 35. Bg5+ { Check! And behind his king on that diagonal sits his rook. Whichever way he steps, your bishop takes the rook next. [%cal Gg5f6,Gf6d8] } (35. a4 { A quiet pawn move, and his king steps off the diagonal. } 35... Ke6 { The moment has gone, and with his pawns so far advanced he is slightly better. [%csl Ge6] }) (35. h4 { A pawn move while his king stood on the diagonal, and it simply walks away. } 35... Ke6 { His king is safe, his pawns are strong, and he is better. [%csl Ge6] }) (35. Rxd3 { You grab a pawn, and his rook takes yours. [%cal Rd8d3] } 35... Rxd3 { A rook for a pawn. He is winning. [%csl Rd3] }) 35... Ke6 { His king steps aside, and his rook is left alone at the far end of the diagonal. } 36. Bxd8 { A whole rook for nothing. Check the king first, then take what was hiding behind him: that is the skewer. [%csl Gd8] } (36. Bd2 { Your bishop retreats, and the rook you had won simply escapes. } 36... b5 37. b3 Kd5 { His rook is safe and his king is marching. He is much better. [%csl Rd8] }) (36. h4 { A pawn move, and his rook simply leaves the diagonal. } 36... Rc8 { The rook is safe, and he is better. [%csl Rc8] }) (36. Rxd3 { Your rook takes the pawn, and his rook takes yours, stepping out of your bishop's reach at the same time. [%cal Rd8d3] } 36... Rxd3 { A rook for a pawn, and the rook you could have taken is gone. He is winning. [%csl Rd3] }) 1-0


