{ You are Black, and you are in trouble: his pawn is nearly home, and his knight stands in your bishop's way. [%csl Ra6] }
41. Ke3 { His king steps back to attack your rook, and that is the mistake: it should have marched toward his pawn. Your rook is attacked, but running is not the answer. } 41... Rf3+ { Check! Your rook is safe there, because your bishop guards it, and on the same row behind his king stands his queen. [%cal Gc6f3,Gf3e3,Ge3b3] } (41... Rf6 { You run with the rook, and his king and his pawn roll on. } 42. Kd4 Be8 43. Qd3+ { His pawn is nearly a queen and his queen is checking. He is winning. [%csl Ra6] }) (41... Rh2 { The rook attacks a pawn, and his king simply walks forward. } 42. Kd4 Kh6 43. Qxf7 { His queen takes a pawn, and his own pawn is still two steps from a new queen. He is winning. [%csl Rf7] }) (41... Bxb7 { You grab the knight, but his pawn takes back and is one step from a new queen. [%cal Ra6b7] } 42. axb7 { Nothing stops that pawn now. He is winning. [%csl Rb7] }) (41... Re2+ { A check, but his king simply takes the rook. [%cal Re3e2] } 42. Kxe2 { A rook for nothing. He is winning. [%csl Re2] }) 42. Kd4 { His king steps off the row, and his queen is left standing at the far end of it, with nothing guarding her. } 42... Rxb3 { A whole queen for nothing. The check came first, and his queen was what stood behind the king: that is the skewer. [%csl Gb3] } (42... Kg8 { A king move, and his queen escapes with a threat of her own. } 43. Qb6 Rf4+ 44. Kc5 { His pawn is coming and his queen is free. He is winning. [%csl Ra6] }) (42... Rf4+ { Another check, but this time nothing stands behind his king, and his queen gets away. } 43. Ke5 Rf6 44. a7 { His pawn is one step from a new queen, and nothing can stop it. He is winning. [%csl Ra7] }) (42... Bxb7 { You take the knight, and his queen checks first, then his pawn takes back. } 43. Qb1+ g6 44. axb7 { The pawn is one step from a new queen. He is winning. [%csl Rb7] }) 0-1


