35... Qd7 { His queen steps across to attack your bishop, and that is the mistake: he should have traded queens. [%cal Rd7d3] } 36. Qh7+ { Check! His king must step out, and it has only one square. [%cal Gh7g8] } (36. Ra1 { You go for his back row with the rook, and he has time to tidy up. } 36... g6 37. Ra8 Qd8 38. Rxb8 Qxb8 { The rooks came off and nothing of his hangs. The position is level. [%csl Gb8] }) (36. Qf5 { You offer the queens off, and he accepts. } 36... Qxf5 37. Bxf5 { No queens, no attack. The position is level. [%csl Gf5] }) (36. Qe8+ { A check straight into his rook, and the rook takes your queen. [%cal Rb8e8] } 36... Rxe8 { A queen for nothing. You are lost. [%csl Re8] }) 36... Kf8 { His king slides along his back row, away from your queen. } 37. Qh8+ { Check again, along his back row, and at the far end of it stands his rook. Wherever the king goes, the rook is next. [%cal Gh8f8,Gf8b8] } (37. Ra1 { You bring the rook over, and he uses the move to aim his bishop at yours. } 37... Bc4 38. Qh8+ Ke7 39. Qxb8 Qxd3+ { You won the rook after all, but his queen took your bishop with check. The position is level. [%csl Gd3] }) (37. Rf1 { A rook move, and his bishop comes to the middle to trade queens. } 37... Bc4 38. Rf4 Qxd3+ 39. Qxd3 Bxd3 40. Kxd3 Rxb2 { The queens and bishops came off, and his rook ate your pawn. A pawn down, with his rook deep in your camp, he is winning. [%csl Rb2] }) (37. Qe4 { Your queen retreats, and the attack is over. } 37... Be6 { His pieces are safe, and he is better. [%csl Ge6] }) (37. Qxg7+ { You grab a pawn with check, and his king takes your queen. [%cal Rf8g7] } 37... Kxg7 { A queen for a pawn. You are lost. [%csl Rg7] }) 37... Ke7 { His king steps off the back row to escape the check, and his rook stays behind, unguarded. } 38. Qxb8 { A whole rook. Two checks drove his king along the row, and the rook was waiting at the end of it: that is the skewer. [%csl Gb8] } (38. Qxg7 { You take a pawn instead of the rook, and his pieces wake up. } 38... Be6 39. Rf1 Qb7 { His rook is safe and his queen guards everything. The position is level. [%csl Gb8] }) (38. Qh7 { The queen steps back, and his bishop comes to the middle. } 38... Bc4 { The rook is safe, and he is slightly better. [%csl Gc4] }) (38. Qd8+ { A check into his rook, and the rook takes your queen. [%cal Rb8d8] } 38... Rxd8 { A queen for nothing, and his pieces are coming for your king. You are lost. [%csl Rd8] }) (38. Rc1 { Your rook goes to the open column while his rook takes your queen. [%cal Rb8h8] } 38... Rxh8 { Your queen is gone, and you have nothing for her. You are lost. [%csl Rh8] }) 1-0


