{ You are White, and we will now learn to remove the defender. Take the guard off the board first, then take the prize. }
28... Rc8 { His rook lands on your column, facing your queen and rook, and that is a mistake: his queen is the only piece guarding it. Take the guard first: your queen takes his queen. [%csl Rc8][%cal Ge6c8,Yc4e6] } 29. Qxe6 { Queen for queen, and the guard is gone. His rook on your column has nobody left to look after it. [%cal Gc1c8] } (29. axb4 { You grab a pawn, and his rook drops onto your queen instead. } 29... Rxc4 30. dxc4 axb4 { His rook ate your queen and you only won a rook back. A queen against your rook is simply lost. [%csl Rc4] }) (29. Rc2 { A rook move that changes nothing: he takes your queen anyway, because a rook is a cheap price for a queen. } 29... Rxc4 30. dxc4 { Your queen is gone for his rook, and his queen alone is stronger than your rook and pawns. [%csl Rc4] }) 29... fxe6 { He takes back with the pawn. The guard is off the board and his rook stands there with nobody to help it. Take it with your rook, with check. [%cal Yc1c8] } 30. Rxc8+ { A whole rook, with check, and it cost you nothing more. Remove the defender first, then collect the prize. } (30. Rb1 { You forget the rook that your queen was spent to win, and he gets a completely free move. } 30... bxa3 31. Kxa3 { Level: his pawn took yours, and the queen you spent bought you nothing at all. [%csl Ra3] }) 1-0


