{ You are Black. Time to remove a defender: your rook and his knight are on one column, with your own knight standing between them. Something is guarding that knight of his. }
19. Nh2 { His knight steps back to the edge, and that is a mistake: it was one of the guards, and it has left. Ask what is still defending the knight your rook is aimed at. } 19... Nxe3 { Take the guard. With his other knight away, his bishop was the only thing defending the knight on your column. [%cal Ge3d2] } (19... Rd7 { Your rook shuffles down the column and still cannot see past your own knight, so nothing has changed. } 20. Nhf1 Rhd8 21. Nb3 { He has untangled and his extra knight counts. He is winning, and the moment has gone. [%csl Rb3] }) (19... b4 { A pawn push that does not touch his knight at all, and it costs you the one moment you had. } 20. Nhf1 { He untangles his pieces, and a knight up for one pawn he is winning. [%csl Rf1] }) (19... Nxc3 { Your knight grabs a pawn, and his pawn takes it straight back. [%cal Rb2c3] } 20. bxc3 { A knight for a pawn. He is winning. [%csl Rc3] }) (19... Bf7 { A quiet bishop move, and it gives him the time he needs to untangle. } 20. Nhf1 Rhe8 21. a3 { His knights are safe and his extra piece counts. He is winning. [%csl Rf1] }) 20. Rxe3 { He takes back with his rook, and now the knight your rook is staring at has nobody at all looking after it. } 20... Rxd2 { A free knight! You gave a knight for his bishop, then collected the one left with no guard: remove the defender first. } (20... Rd5 { You step the rook forward instead of taking, and that one move is all his loose knight needs to get away. } 21. Ne4 Bg6 22. Nf1 { His knight was there for the taking and you let it walk away. Nothing at all is left of the idea. [%csl Rf1] }) (20... Rd4 { Your rook steps onto a square his pawn attacks, and the pawn takes it. [%cal Rc3d4] } 21. cxd4 { A whole rook gone for nothing. He is winning. [%csl Rd4] }) (20... Rd3 { Your rook attacks his, but his rook takes yours first. [%cal Re3d3] } 21. Rxd3 { His rook ate yours, and you have nothing to show for it. He is winning. [%csl Rd3] }) (20... Bg6 { Your bishop steps back, and he gets the one move he needed to guard the loose knight. } 21. Nhf1 Kb7 22. a4 { His knights are safe and he is a knight up for a pawn. He is winning. [%csl Rd2] }) 0-1


