{ You are White. His bishop is tying your knight down, and your pawn in the middle is attacked. }
15... Nxe5 { His knight takes your pawn, and that is the mistake: his bishop had to step back instead. Now he is a pawn up, and he feels safe. Look again. } 16. Nxe5 { Your knight takes his knight. It was the only piece guarding his bishop, so whatever he does, a piece of his is about to fall. [%cal Ge2g4] } (16. Nh4 { Your knight runs from the pin, and his bishop steps away safely. } 16... Bh3 { He keeps his extra pawn and is slightly better. [%csl Rh3] }) (16. a4 { A pawn move on the other wing, and his bishop steps back. } 16... Bf5 { His bishop is safe, and a pawn up he is slightly better. [%csl Rf5] }) (16. c3 { A quiet move, and his knight retreats to safety. } 16... Ng6 { His pieces are safe, he is a pawn up, and he is better. [%csl Rg6] }) 16... Bxe5 { His bishop takes your knight back, and his other bishop is left with nobody to guard it. } 17. Bxg4 { A free bishop. His knight was the guard, so you took the guard first and then the prize: that is removing the defender. [%csl Gg4] } (17. c3 { A quiet move, and he trades the bishop off himself. } 17... Bxe2 18. Qxe2 { He keeps his extra pawn, and he is slightly better. [%csl Re2] }) (17. Rb1 { A rook move, and his bishop simply steps back. } 17... Bf5 { He is a pawn up and better. [%csl Rf5] }) (17. Qb1 { Your queen slides to the side, and his bishop trades itself off. } 17... Bxe2 18. Rxe2 { The bishops came off, and a pawn up he is better. [%csl Re2] }) 1-0


