{ You are Black, three pawns down, and his rooks have broken into your camp. It looks hopeless. Look again. }
42. Be5 { His bishop comes to the middle, and that is the mistake: it was his rook that belonged on that square. He expects to win easily. } 42... Nxd5 { Your knight takes his rook! That rook was the only guard of his bishop, so when his pawn takes back, the bishop is left alone. [%cal Ge6e5] } (42... b5 { A pawn push, and his rooks swing into action. } 43. Rdd7 { Two rooks on the seventh row, three pawns up. Even if you take the bishop, he takes your knight back: he is winning. [%csl Rd7] }) (42... Re8 { Your other rook comes to the same column, and his rook comes forward. } 43. Rd6 { His rooks dominate, three pawns up. He is winning. [%csl Rd6] }) (42... Rxe5 { You take the bishop first, and his rook takes your rook. [%cal Rd5e5] } 43. Rxe5 { A rook for a bishop, three pawns down. He is winning. [%csl Re5] }) 43. cxd5 { His pawn takes your knight back, and now his bishop stands in the middle with nobody guarding it. } 43... Rxe5 { A whole bishop. Your knight took the rook that guarded it, then your rook took the bishop: that is removing the defender. A rook up for three pawns, and now you are the one winning. [%csl Ge5] } (43... Ree8 { Your rook retreats, and he gets time to guard the bishop with a pawn. } 44. f4 { His bishop is safe and his pawns are strong. He is winning. [%csl Rd5] }) (43... Kf5 { Your king comes forward, and his pawn takes your rook. [%cal Rd5e6] } 44. dxe6 { His pawn took your rook. A piece and three pawns up, he is winning. [%csl Re6] }) (43... Rf6 { Your rook attacks only a guarded pawn, and his bishop takes it. } 44. Bxf6 { You can take the bishop back, but a rook for a bishop, three pawns down, he is winning. [%csl Rf6] }) 0-1
